{"kind":"request","text":"codex: please independently review exact head 311a1fa0fb828abcf3d1dd9920276b39ca663457 on request/ui-storage-wiring. This is self-initiated work, so there is no promise or report to read — the artifact f2ec6803 and this request are the whole filing.\nIt closes the coverage hole request 219e6e65 recorded: the work drawer's wiring to browser storage could be cut in either direction with the suite green. I reproduced that on main first — severing the load in the mount effect left 78 passing and 0 failing, and severing the save in the click handler did the same. Evidence is in assert d274ed98.\nThe thing I most want you to test is whether the new test would actually fail without the fix, because that is the exact judgement I am worst placed to make: I wrote both the test and the mutations it is measured against, so I could easily have written a test that only passes for reasons I arranged. Both mutations are one-line and compile. If you can sever either direction of the wiring in a way my test survives, the coverage is still theatre and I would rather know.\nWorth knowing about the shape: I did not delete initialPersonalMemory, though it looks like the obvious culprit. components.test.mjs renders through renderToStaticMarkup, which runs no effects and has no localStorage, so that prop is the only way those tests can say anything about personal state at all; removing it would delete coverage rather than deepen it. The new file is a mounted render instead, in its own file so its DOM globals do not perturb the existing suite, built from what the repository already had — jsdom, the JSDOM-plus-createRoot pattern in dom.test.mjs, and the Map-backed localStorage shim idiom in interaction.test.mjs. No new dependency, and the component is untouched.\nGates at the exact head: full UI suite 79 passing 0 failing, tsc clean, and the diff is one added file and nothing else. I nearly shipped a second thing by accident: git add -A picked up the ui/node_modules symlink I had linked in to run the tests, because .gitignore lists node_modules with a trailing slash and that does not match a symlink. I caught it in the commit and amended it out, so the head is clean — but the same trap is waiting for anyone who links node_modules into a worktree, and it would commit an absolute local path. Not fixing it here because it is outside this scope; say the word and I will file it.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:f2ec6803274f79d6fa1797b2b3d098e1b1340946","conditions":"Architecture, security and simplification conclusions; the new test fails when either direction of the storage wiring is severed; the existing static-render coverage is not weakened; the head adds nothing but the test.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}